CORE
main.cpp
--cpp17 --show-symbol-table
^EXIT=0$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^CONVERSION ERROR$
^.*found no match for symbol 'vector'.*$
^.*is initialized with constant.*$
--
Verifies two fixes that are both required for compiling typical
libstdc++-using code with `std::vector<X>{...}` brace-init:

  * `cpp_typecheck.cpp`'s `_S_use_relocate`/`_S_nothrow_relocate`
    override wraps the constant in a `code_block { return; }`
    rather than assigning a bare `true_exprt()`.  Without the
    wrap, `convert_function`'s `symbol.value.id() == ID_code`
    precondition fires.

  * `typecheck_expr_explicit_constructor_call` keeps a
    brace-init-list as a single operand when T has a non-explicit
    `initializer_list<U>` constructor (phase 2.1 of
    [over.match.list]/2), only expanding it for phase 2.2 when no
    init-list ctor is present.

`--show-symbol-table` exits cleanly after typechecking so we don't
trip BMC-time "no body for callee" failures on libstdc++ internal
methods (`_M_range_initialize`, `__uninit_default_n`,
`operatorcpp_delete`) that are unrelated to typecheck-time
correctness.  The negative patterns ensure none of the three
critical typecheck-time error chains regress.
